# Build output. Nothing here is source.
*.o
/satl
/satl.haswell
/satl-cpu-level
/satl-term
.cxxflags-stamp
.cxxflags-stamp-haswell
.ldflags-stamp

# The empty program `make startup` measures satl against. A build artefact like
# the four above and not a source: 067-startup.mk generates `int main(){return
# 0;}` on the compiler's stdin, so there is no .cpp for this to shadow.
.startup-floor

# The test binaries, which live BESIDE their sources rather than in a build
# directory, so this cannot be a directory exclusion -- tests/words_test/ is
# source and must stay tracked.
#
# IGNORE EVERYTHING IN A TEST FOLDER, THEN NAME WHAT IS SOURCE. The other way
# round -- listing the binaries -- was written first and was wrong: 065-tests.mk
# builds tests/<name>/<name>, so the pattern would have to repeat every test's
# name, and the comment above it claimed adding a test needed no edit here while
# the line below it said otherwise. This way adding a test really does need no
# edit, which is the same property TESTNAMES has.
#
# A test that ever needs a DATA file beside its sources needs a `!` line for it,
# and that is a real edit. Said here rather than found later, when the file is
# missing from a fresh clone and the test fails for a reason nothing names.
/tests/*/*
!/tests/*/*.cpp
!/tests/*/*.hpp

# The prototype test binaries, which sit beside their sources exactly as the real
# ones do -- so this is the rule above, applied to a second directory for the
# same reason and written the same way round.
#
# THE SOURCES ARE TRACKED ON PURPOSE. MILESTONES/M3.md §3 and MILESTONES/M4.md §3
# each cite a directory under here by path and say what it got wrong; a citation
# to a file that is not in the repository is a citation to nothing. What is not
# tracked is the binary each prototype builds -- and `*.o` at the top of this
# file already covers the objects beside them.
/prototype/*/*
!/prototype/*/*.cpp
!/prototype/*/*.hpp
!/prototype/*/*.def
!/prototype/*/*.md
!/prototype/*/Makefile

# The first satellite, kept beside this one to read and to pull from, and
# DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY'S HISTORY. It is a whole second tree --
# 371 tracked files, its own build, its own design/, plus built binaries and
# tarballs -- and committing it here would double the repository to carry a
# copy of something that already has its own history somewhere else.
#
# It is a REFERENCE, not a dependency. Nothing under src/ includes from it,
# nothing in the build reaches into it, and satl compiles with the folder
# absent. PLAN_ONE.md cites paths inside it the way it cites a book: if the
# folder is not there, the citation is still true and you go and find the tree.
#
# Trailing slash, so this ignores the DIRECTORY and cannot be satisfied by a
# file that happens to be called old_versions. Leading slash, so it is anchored
# at the repository root and an old_versions/ somewhere deeper is still yours to
# decide about.
/old_versions/

# Python's bytecode cache, which help_lines/ makes the moment gen.py imports
# topics.py. Generated, machine-specific, and never worth a diff.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The binary package under distribute/, which is BUILT and not source.
#
# distribute/make-package.sh fills enterprise_satellite/ with four compiled
# programs, the artwork, the examples and the hicolor index.theme, and then
# writes a tarball beside it. Every one of those is either a build artefact --
# same category as /satl at the top of this file -- or a COPY of something this
# repository already tracks somewhere else.
#
# THE COPIES ARE THE REASON share/ AND example/ ARE HERE and not just programs/.
# share/icons and share/applications are copied from satellite_enterprise/icons,
# and example/ from /example, both by that script and both verified with cmp on
# the way. Committing the copies would mean two tracked versions of the same
# eighteen PNGs, which is precisely the drift this file refuses everywhere else.
# The originals stay tracked; the copies are made on demand.
#
# WHAT IS STILL SOURCE, AND IS STILL TRACKED: install-satellite.sh, the ten
# fragments in install_support/, READ-ME-FIRST.txt, README.md and
# make-package.sh. Those are the package -- what it ships is regenerated by
# running it.
/distribute/enterprise_satellite/programs/
/distribute/enterprise_satellite/share/
/distribute/enterprise_satellite/example/
/distribute/*.tar.gz
